{"schema_version": "0.1", "cited_work": {"id": "doi:10.1093/poq/nfs038", "title": "Affect, Not Ideology: A Social Identity Perspective on Polarization"}, "citing_work": {"id": "doi:10.1111/hcre.12028"}, "method": {"name": "author-year", "version": "1/pdfminer-20260107/ftfy-6.3.1", "parameters": {"author": "Iyengar", "year": "2012"}}, "passages": [{"id": "p3-char815", "location": {"pdf_page": 3}, "quote": "Affective polarization in the U.S. stands in contrast to other countries such as the U.K., which have not seen comparable changes in attitudes toward oppositional parties (Iyengar et al., 2012).", "review": "unchecked"}, {"id": "p3-char1132", "location": {"pdf_page": 3}, "quote": "Tajfel & Turner, 1979), appears to be more robust than for polarization grounded in citizens' issue positions or policy preferences (Iyengar et al., 2012).", "review": "unchecked"}, {"id": "p15-char1614", "location": {"pdf_page": 15}, "quote": "The first was displeasure at the prospect of one's offspring marrying an out-group member (Almond & Verba, 1963; Bogardus, 1947; Iyengar et al., 2012).", "review": "unchecked"}, {"id": "p24-char1680", "location": {"pdf_page": 24}, "quote": "This is despite the distinct political and media systems in the two countries, and some indications that increased affective polarization at the society level may be specific to recent U.S. history (Iyengar et al., 2012).", "review": "unchecked"}], "source": {"kind": "pdf", "location": "testdata/15.pdf", "sha256": "ad74e3586079239d9066199e7c53735f96b7aa548063b381d0813fac7484ee07"}, "outcome": "passages_found"}
